    "summary": "Stellar Peggy‑O, soaring Eyes→Morning Dew jam, tight second set",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Eyes → Morning Dew climax and a first‑time Peggy‑O that crackles with Jerry’s peak guitar work. The second set tightens into seamless transitions, highlighted by China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider and a powerful Weather Report Suite. Recording quality is crystal‑clear on the remastered SBD, making it an essential listen.",
    "review": "On December 12, 1973 at the Omni in Atlanta, the Dead turned in one of their most exhilarating late‑year performances. Jerry Garcia was unmistakably “at his peak,” laying down a soaring lead tone that lit up the iconic Eyes → Morning Dew jam and added soft lilting runs on the ballad‑like finale. Phil Lesh supplied massive bass bombs—especially before the bridge of Morning Dew—and owned the low end despite a brief, likely headphone‑related drop‑out. Bob Weir’s rhythm work and vocal harmonies kept the groove solid, while Keith Godchaux’s organ added texture to the Weather Report Suite and Looks Like Rain. The second set was a masterclass in flow: Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo segued into Me And Bobby McGee, then into a flawless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider pair that kept the energy foot‑stomping, with Bill Kreutzmann’s drumming driving the band forward at a slightly faster tempo than later shows. The remastered soundboard capture (gd1973-12-12.sbd.remaster.102910.flac16) presents this magic in crystal‑clear detail—each instrument breathes, and the occasional bass dip is clearly a monitoring issue, not a recording flaw. For anyone looking to experience a high‑energy 1973 set with superb audio fidelity, this show stands out as a must‑listen.",
